Non-random variables with multiple constant or non-constant drivers are not supported.
Passing a Randomized Object Through a Function/Task Argument
Example 37 shows how you can use randomized object arguments to target a non-random
variable associated coverpoints.
Example 37
class OtherClass;
rand int ri1;
program test;
int i1;
cp1 : coverpoint (i1);

task my_sample(OtherClass oo);
i1 = oo.ri1;
enclass
MyClass obj1 = new;
OtherClass oo = new;
repeat(5)
oo.randomize;
obj1.my_sample(oo);
end
end
Multiple Drivers Present in Different Methods (Functions/Tasks)
Echo identifies dead code and omits the drivers present in dead code in the presence of
multiple
drivers
multiple drivers. Echo does not substitute the sampled variable by the driver of type rand.
You need to check the callers progressively until the root caller is reached. However, if the
sampling function is virtual or the function calling the sampling function is virtual, you can
resolve whether the function is called or not only at runtime. Echo cannot do this analysis
at compile time to figure out if there is dead code.
In cases where the sampling function is non-virtual, you may check using the -ntb_opts
echo_single_scope_check switch whether the sampling function is called or not rather
than checking the entire caller chain. This is helpful where the non-virtual sampling
function is called within a virtual function.
In Example 38, the my_sample2 task is dead code as it is not being called. In effect, there
is only one driver on the i1 variable.
Example 38
class OtherClass;
rand int ri1;
int i1;
cp1 : coverpoint (i1) {
bins s0 = {0};
bins s1 = {1};
bins s2 = {2};
bins s3 = {3};
}

task my_sample1(OtherClass oo);
i1 = oo.ri1 + 1;

task my_sample2(OtherClass oo);
i1 = oo.ri1 + 3;

MyClass obj = new;
OtherClass oo = new;

repeat(5)
oo.randomize;
obj.my_sample1(oo);
$display("i1=%d", obj.i1);
end
end
