Example: Coverpoint on a Real Expression
covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
cp: coverpoint my_real_signal {
bins low = {[0.0:1.0]};
bins mid = {[1.0:2.0]};
bins high = {[2.0:3.0]};
}
In this example, the coverpoint cp is defined on the real-valued signal my_real_signal,
and explicit bins are provided to cover different ranges.
When you define a coverpoint on a real expression, you must explicitly specify at least one
user-defined bin. This is because bins are not automatically created for coverpoints of real
expressions.
If you do not specify at least one explicit user bin for a real-valued coverpoint, the following
compile error is issued:
Error-[FCNOBNRCP] No User Bin in Real Valued Coverpoint
test.sv, 4
top, "top::cg"
User bin is not present in coverpoint cp of covergroup top::cg.
Coverpoint of real expression must specify at least one explicit bins
construct.
This error is also issued if only illegal or ignore bins are present in a real-valued
coverpoint.
Bins of Real-Valued Coverpoint
User-Defined Bins
You can explicitly specify the ranges or values for bins. This is useful when you want to
track coverage for specific intervals or values.
covergroup temp_cg @(posedge clk);
temp_cp: coverpoint temperature {
bins freezing = {[ -40.0 : 0.0 ]};
bins cold = {[ 0.0 : 10.0 ]};
bins warm = {[ 10.0 : 25.0 ]};
bins hot = {[ 25.0 : 50.0 ]};
}
In this example, the coverpoint temp_cp is defined on the real-valued signal temperature,
with bins covering different temperature ranges: freezing, cold, warm, and hot.
Sample URG Report

Bin Name Range Covered Percent
low [0.0:1.0] Yes 100%
mid [1.0:2.0] No 0%
high [2.0:3.0] Yes 100%
Auto Bins
Auto bins partition a range into intervals automatically, based on the type option for real
intervals.
covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
cp: coverpoint real_val {
bins auto[] = {[0.0:3.0]};
}
If type_option.real_interval is set to 1.0, this creates three bins: [0.0:1.0),
[1.0:2.0), [2.0:3.0].
Sample URG Report
Bin Name Range Covered Percent
auto[0] [0.0:1.0) Yes 100%
auto[1] [1.0:2.0) No 0%
auto[2] [2.0:3.0] Yes 100%
Cross Coverage
You can cross two coverpoints to see which combinations of bins are hit.
covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
cp1: coverpoint real_val1 {
bins a[] = {[0.0:2.0]};
}
cp2: coverpoint real_val2 {
bins b[] = {[1.0:3.0]};
}
cross_cp: cross cp1, cp2;

Callback Functions
This topic describes the callback functions:
Randomize Callback Function
Simulation triggers callbacks at four different time points: before/after pre_randomize and
before/after post_randomize.
int rand_callback_main (solver_event_e event, handle_type sv_clsobj,
handle_type ctxt);
Argument: event
BeginPreRandomizeC, //in the beginning of pre_randomize
EndPreRandomizeC, //in the end of pre_randomize
BeginPostRandomizeC,//in the beginning of post_randomize
EndPostRandomizeC //in the end of post_randomize
EndRandomizeC //at the end of randomize() and before
post_randomize() call.
} solver_event_e;
sv_clsobj
SV class object in simv.
ctxt
Context object in simv.
Callback function returns different values which are defined in $VCS_HOME/include/
constraint/cuml/runtime_user.hh.
cumlCBFailC, // Solve fails and proceed without re-try.
cumlCBSucceedC, // Solve succeeds and will proceed with simulation
without re-try.
cumlCBRetryC, // Retry randomize.
cumlCBRetryAndTraceOnC, // Retry randomize and enable the solver
trace for constraint inconsistency.
} cumlCallBackRetType;
The return status being assigned in the body of callback function is used to decide if
re-randomize is needed when the current callback is triggered at the end of randomize
(means that the mode is EndRandomizeC. Re-randomize occurs until the return value is
changed to cumlCBSucceedC.

• BeginPreRandomizeC/EndPreRandomizeC is for the controller. If constraints and
variables are modified at these two phases, the current randomization is impacted.
You must check the event type in your C++ code. Multiple callbacks could be triggered
in different places for pre_randomize.
• BeginPostRandomizeC/EndPostRandomizeC is only for observation. If constraints and
variables are modified at these two phases, the next randomization is impacted.
You must check the event type in your C++ code. Multiple callbacks could be triggered
in different places for post_randomize.
• EndRandomizeC is used for re-randomize. You can decide if re-try is needed
or not by checking the return status of the current randomize (for example,
Context::get_randomize_return_status()).
Sim Callback Function
int sim_callback_main (cuml_sim_event_e mode, handle_type
context_handle);
This callback function is used to query or initialize an object design in the beginning
of simulation or for statistics or summary at the end of the simulation. Simv triggers
callbacks at two difference places, which is corresponding to the different values of the first
argument mode as follows:
Argument: mode
BeginSimulationC, //in the beginning of simulation
EndSimulationC, //at the end of simulation
} siml_sim_event_e;
context_handle
Context object in simv.
There are other API interfaces related to the callback functions:
int rand_callback_get_mode (solver_event_e event)
void rand_callback_set_mode (solver_event_e event, int on_or_off)
These methods are used to control whether callback function is enabled or disabled in
pre_randomize(), post_randomize(), and randomize(). rand_callback_get_mode()
returns the current state of callback function call with the given parameter. Work globally
on and off, and independently for observation and override.
Disable Callback Function
bool Context::disable_callback_in_subtree()

Disable callback function is called from the root object's subtree when there are random
children under the root object starting from randomize() call.
