Static and Dynamic Linking
The main difference between static and dynamic linking is the time at which the object
files are loaded into an application program. In case of static linking, object files are linked
during elaboration, and become part of the simulation executable (simv). In case of
dynamic linking, loading is performed at runtime.
Static Linking in VCS
You can compile C/C++/SystemC files into object files and archive them in a common
object file (.a's), as follows:
% g++ -03 -Wall -I. -c ext_inv.cpp -o ext_inv.o
% g++ -03 -Wall -I. -c ext_buf.cpp -o ext_buf.o
% ar -r extenv.a ext_inv.o ext_buf.o
Add the -I${VCS_HOME}/include/systemc_version option to C/C++ compiler
to compile SystemC files.
The archive can be statically linked by only passing the archive on the vcs command line.
% vcs top ./extenv.a
Where, top is the top level module name.
If the object file is for SystemC, add the -sysc option to the vcs command line.

// (1) compile source file, link to simulation
// (2) create SC/HDL interface model
% syscan A.cpp:A

