Specify the -sc_portmap option to parse the portmap file, as shown in the following
command line:
% vhdlan -sc_model myVHDL myVHDL.vhdl -sc_portmap myVHDL.map
The portmap file identifies the ports that are to be bundled into the sc_vector construct.
For information about creating and using the portmap file, see section Using the Port Map
File. The following is a sample portmap file:
## myVHDL.map
## portname bits hdl-type sysc-type isvector OSCI-
name
reset_n_i 1 bit bool
rx1_data_I_i_* 64 real double sc_vector
rx1_data_I_i
rx1_data_O_i_* 64 real double sc_vector
rx1_data_O_i

signment. However, this can influence testcases where the signal
transfer has to be with a slight delay, or with an NBA assignment. You can achieve this in
the portmap file by specifying the delay specification. The following are the possible values
that you can provide for delay specification:
• NONE: no special delay is generated and NONE used by default.
always @(Routx_1_D) Routx_1 = Routx_1_D;
assign outx_1 = Routx_1
• ZERO: the assignment is made after a delay of #0 (next delta cycle).
always @(Routx_1_D) Routx_1 = #0 Routx_1_D;
assign outx_1 = Routx_1
• NBA: the assignment is made as a non-blocking assignment and is executed after the
delta queues are executed:
always @(Routx_1_D) Routx_1 <= Routx_1_D;
assign outx_1 = Routx_1
When the portmap file contains delay specifications, then an additional Verilog module
is generated containing these delay implementations. This module must be explicitly
instantiated, instead of the normal Verilog module, as shown in the following code:
module sub3(output [7:0] outx_1);
// default module generated from SystemC module

module sub3_delayed(output [7:0] outx_1);
// delayed module generated from SystemC module
reg [7:0] Routx_1;
wire [7:0] Routx_1_D;
sub3 (Routx_1_D);
always @(Routx_1_D) Routx_1 <= Routx_1_D;
assign outx_1 = Routx_1;
The delay specification is only supported with SystemC-down designs.
Array Expansion

The interface generator connects array-ports automatically to each other. It recognizes
that the port is an array-port. For the SystemC module, it recognizes that the port is a
pointer to an array of ports.
To avoid the default mapping to array-ports, you can use the expand attribute in the
portmap file to expand the array port on the SystemC side into a set of separate ports.
Array-ports on the Verilog side are not expanded.
This attribute is only valid for DPI-based interfaces, that is, it is required to generate the
interface with the -sysc=dpi_if option.
For a SystemC-on-top, Verilog-down configuration, where the interface is created from the
Verilog file, the following array interface is created, by default:
Input file: v_array.v:
module v_array(array_in, array_out);
input [7:0] array_in[2:0];
output [7:0] array_out[2:0];
…
The following is the SystemC module that is generated:
Generated file: csrc/sysc/v_array/v_array.h
SC_MODULE(v_array) {
sc_in<sc_int<8> > *array_in[3];
sc_out<sc_int<8> > *array_out[3];
…
};
To expand the array on the SystemC side, the expand attribute is used in the portmap file:
Input file: v_array.map
array_in 8 bitvector sc_int expand
array_out 8 bitvector sc_int expand
The corresponding SystemC module is defined as follows:
Generated file: csrc/sysc/v_array/v_array.h
