Access in SV code:
int a;
a = sc_inst0.get_DDD();
Arrays
Arrays are also supported. Individual elements can be accessed if the type of the element
is generally supported. Accessing entire arrays, or sub-arrays (rows, columns) is not
supported.
Read or write access takes place with the get or set SV function, if the indexes are
specified as 2nd, 3rd, and so on arguments.
SystemC instance definition:
SC_MODULE(ABC)
{

int BBB[10];
bool CCC[1024,500];

};
Usage of TLI adaptor in SV code:
a = sc_inst0.get_BBB(7); // read BBB[7]
b = sc_inst0.get_CCC(700,2); // read CCC[700,2]
sc_inst0.set_CCC(!b,700,2); // write CCC[700,2]
SC_FIFO
The sc_fifo class can be accessed if the template argument type is supported. The
access functions permit non-blocking access (see the SystemC LRM, sc_fifo, methods
nb_read, sc_fifo::nb_write), and support queries for the number of free or stored
elements.
Access to read() and write() blocking functions is not supported.
Definition of SystemC instance:
SC_MODULE(ABC)

sc_fifo<int> FFF;

};
Access in SV code:
int a, num;
num = sc_inst0.get_FFF_num_available();

num = sc_inst0.get_FFF_num_free();
a= sc_inst0.get_FFF(); //function, will not block
sc_inst0.set_FFF(a); //function, will not block
Non-SystemC Classes
A SystemC module definition is a C++ class derived from sc_module. It is often specified
with a SC_MODULEmacro.
All SystemC modules are C++ classes, but not all C++ classes are SystemC modules. The
C++ classes that are not SystemC modules are referred to as non-SystemC-classes.
Accessing the members of non-SystemC-classes is not supported. The top-level class
must be sc_module derived class.
Sub-Classes
A class may have a member which itself is a class. Members of such sub-classes can
also be accessed (if they are to be imported, see the following TLI file). Members of sub-
classes, or sub-sub-classes are accessed using the SV functions in the TLI adaptor that
reflect the C++ scope name.
Definition of C++ classes:
class C2
{
int P;
int Q;
};

struct C1
{
int M;
C2 N;
};

SC_MODULE(ABC)
{

int my_meth();
int AAA;
C1 SSS;

};
Usage in SV code:
// create a TLI adapter instance
tli_ABC sc_inst0;

// call SC method my_meth()
my_int = sc_inst0.my_meth();

// read SC member ABC::AAA
a = sc_inst0.get_AAA();

// write '12' to SC member ABC::AAA
sc_inst0.set_AAA(12);

// read SC member ABC::SSS.M
a = sc_inst0.get_SSS_get_M();

// alternate read access to ABC::SSS.M
a = sc_inst0.SSS.get_M();

// write value 'a+12' to SC member ABC::SSS.N.Q
sc_inst0.set_SSS_set_N_set_Q(a+12);

// alternate write to ABC::SSS.N.Q
sc_inst0.SSS.N.set_Q(a+12);
Only the sub-classes instantiated as regular members are supported. The sub-classes that
are connected to the main class as pointers or arrays are not accessible.
Definition of C++ classes:
struct C1
{
int M;
C2 N;
};

SC_MODULE(ABC)
{

C1 SSS;
C1* TTT;
C1 UUU[4];

};
Members of SSS are accessible, but members of TTT and UUU are not accessible.
Name Clashes
Name clashes can occur in the following two types of scenarios:
• var name clashes with member names.
• var name clashes with method names.

var Name Clashes with Member Names
Consider the following example:
class A {
int b;
};
class Foo {
A a;
int a_get_b;
}
In this case, the SystemVerilog access method for SC members Foo::a.b and
Foo::a_get_b can be get_a_get_b(). To handle this scenario, one of the SC member
variables b of class A or a_get_b of class Foo must be changed slightly.
var Name Clashes with Method Names
Consider the following example:
class Foo
{
int A;
int get_A(); //user defined method
};
Now, the generated access method get_A() for variable A clashes with the user-defined
method get_A(). Note that the name clash in this case is only in the SV domain. The
internal plumbing generated names get_A() (to access A) and call_get_A() (to call
get_A()) are unique.
To handle this case, keep the get_A() method (for calling get_A()) in the SV domain as
is. Change the access method to escaped name \:get_A().
Error Handling
Locating SystemC Instance
The hierarchical path specified as an actual parameter of the TLI adaptor is checked
during the startup of the simulation. An error is reported and the simulation stops when the
path cannot be resolved to a SystemC instance.
Out-of-Array Accesses
Reading or writing an array element depends on the valid data for the indices. Invalid
indices might accidentally go over the allocated area and access unrelated memory
addresses.

This section provides an additional use model to call the SystemC methods from
SystemVerilog:
The syntax is as follows:
<int_ret> = $call_systemc_func("<systemc_instance_path>.<scmember_func
tion>", arg1, arg2, …)
The first argument is a SystemC instance followed by a SystemC method name. The next
arguments are the arguments which need to be passed to the specified SystemC method.
The arguments must be input type and simple integral data type or a logic. The number
and the order of the argument types must be the same as in the SystemC method that is
called.
To create the $system function , the create_pli_functionsdirective must be specified
in the TLI file. The TLI file is provided with the SystemC module to a syscan call.
The following example illustrates usage of the $call_systemc_function API.
Example 27 Example of TLI-file samp.tli
adaptor sc_tb_drv
tli_compile_mode auto
create_pli_functions true
import function func_*
skip member *
