These functions are extensions to the
TLI-2adaptor code
TLI-2 adaptor code generated for the scalar
member access. For backward compatibility reasons, the previous syntax is still
supported.
The following SystemC code example illustrates the usage of the above functions:
struct simple
{
int A;
sc_int<8> B;
};

SC_MODULE(S){
simple P;

}
The content of P member variable of struct type simple is exchanged with SystemVerilog.

SystemVerilog consists of a corresponding simple-struct compliant class. VMM, UVM,
and OVM have the functionality to pack or unpack SV classes as a whole. The SV class
must be compliant to VMM,UVM, and OVM. With the specific `defines coming with VMM,
UVM, or OVM, the byte pack/unpack functionality can be used on the SV side. On the
SystemC side, the struct must be packed/unpacked in VMM, UVM, or OVM compliant way.
The SV class in the example below does not display any VMM, UVM, or OVM specific
directives, which are required for byte packing.
class simple_SV;
int A;
bit[7:0] B;
endclass:simple_SV

// declare a variable of the SV class simple_SV
simple_SV cl1;
// SV byte pack array
logic[7:0]ba[];

tli_S sc();
sc.scSetScopeByName("top.sc_inst1");

// get an entire struct from SC and fill it into class object //cl1;
sc.P.get(ba);
cl1.byte_unpack(ba);

class object cl1

// VMM class unpack call
cl1.byte_unpack(ba);

// UVM/OVM use a different task call
// cl1.unpack_bytes(ba);

// fill an entire struct on the SC side with contents of
// class object cl1
cl1.byte_pack(ba);

// VMM class byte-pack call
cl1.byte_pack(ba);

// UVM/OVM use a different task call
// cl1.pack_bytes(ba);
sc.P.set(ba);

// Individual struct member access
cl1.A = sc.get_P_get_A();
sc.set_P_set_A(cl1.A);

// Alternate individual member access, old syntax still
// supported.
// Both kinds of individual member acces can be used and
// mixed in SV code.
cl1.A = sc.P.get_A();
sc.P.set_A(cl1.A);

mber access
cl1.A = sc.get_P_get_A();
sc.set_P_set_A(cl1.A);

