With the following syscan command, the interface file is generated and the BusModel
source code is compiled.
syscan -tlm2 -debug_access+all BusModel.cpp:BusModel
The provided SV package with tli_upload and tli_download tasks is analyzed with the
following command:
vlogan -sverilog ${VCS_HOME}/etc/systemc/tlm/tli/ tli_packunpack.sv
Verilog Module Using Byte Packing
The Verilog module top is in top.v file, and it includes the generated
tli_vmm_MemAccess.sv VMM file. It imports the tli_packunpack package using
the tli_upload and tli_download tasks. The Verilog module top instantiates the
SystemC module BusModel, and data exchange occurs with byte packing/unpacking of the
MemAccess class.

reg clock;
BusModel ref_model(clock);
MemAccess trans;
pB bytes;
chandle ID;
initial clock=0;
always #50 clock=!clock;
ID = null;
trans = new;

@(posedge clock);
trans.adr = 98;
trans.RW = 1'b1;
trans.len = 5;
trans.data = new[trans.len];
trans.byte_pack( bytes );

// sent the byte buffer to SC with ID 'null'
tli_upload( bytes, ID );

#20 ;

// load the byte buffer sent from SC
tli_download(bytes, null);
trans.byte_unpack(bytes);
for (n=0; n<trans.len; n=n+1)
$display("trans.data[%3d]=%d", n, trans.data[n]);
#100 $finish;
end
endmodule // top
The top.v file is analyzed using the following command:
vlogan -sverilog -ntb_opts rvm top.v
Building Simulation
The simulation simv is then build using the following vcs command:
vcs -sysc -debug_access+all top -timescale=1ns/1ps

Compile Flow (UVM)
Perform the following steps to compile:
1. Generate the UVM class and the C pack/unpack routines. This step does not compile
C sources. The following syscan command generates the UVM-class declaration (with
the uvm_data_member defines) and the pack and unpack functions:
syscan -tli tli_input_file class_header.h
tli_input_file specifies class_name (followed by the class keyword) and if
the UVM and/or class pack/unpack functions are created (create keyword). The
class_header.h file contains the class definition of the class to be packed or
unpacked.
2. Compile the generated pack and unpack functions and the source file containing the
class to be packed or unpacked, after manually correcting the generated files (SV and
C) which contain complex data types. This is done using the following syscan call:
syscan -tlm2 class_source.cpp tli_packunpack_class_name.cpp
3. Generate an interface for the class which packs or unpacks the class. Include the
include statement of the generated header tli_packunpack_class_name.h file, in
the test_class.h file, as follows:
syscan -tlm2 -debug_access+all test_class.cpp:test_class_name
Other syscan calls for files required for building and running the simulation.
4. Compile the provided package with tli_upload or tli_download tasks, as follows:
vlogan -sverilog ${VCS_HOME}/etc/systemc/tlm/tli/tli_packunpack.sv
5. The SV top.v file contains a module which packs or unpacks the data on the SV side.
The top.v file includes the generated tli_uvm_class_name UVM class file. Analyze
the generated tli_uvm_class_name UVM class file with vlogan, as follows:
vlogan -sverilog -ntb_opts uvm top.v
Analyze other SV files required for simulation.
6. Create simulation with the vcs command:
vcs -ntb_opts uvm -sysc -debug_access+all top -timescale=1ns/1ps -o
simv1

Usage Example for Code Generator
The test case in this example consists of a Verilog top module called top and a
SystemC module BusModel. The struct used for byte packing is called MemAccess.
The memaccess.h file contains the user-defined struct MemAccess, and the TLI file
memaccess.tli contains create statements. The TLI file contains the following
commands:
class MemAccess
create UVM
The memaccess.h file contains the following:
#ifndef MEMACCESS_H
#define MEMACCESS_H 1
struct MemAccess {
unsigned int adr; // address
bool RW; // true=read, false=write
unsigned char* data; // data[len] to be read or written
int len; // number of bytes
};
Code Generation
The struct contains a dynamic array, data. You must manually modify the generated code,
if you want to pack this struct member.
