Support for Default Assignment Pattern for Dynamic Array/Queue
VCS supports default assignment pattern on dynamic array/queue under the -xlrm
vsa_default_ap compile option.
% vcs -sverilog <file_name> -xlrm vsa_default_ap
The assignment pattern on RHS with the default clause does not resize the dynamic
array/queue on LHS. It maps or assigns the default value to the existing elements of the
dynamic array/queue.
The following is the example:
Example 139 test.sv
int dyn_arr[];
int q1[$];
dyn_arr = new[3];
$display("dyn_array elements:",dyn_arr);
dyn_arr = '{default: 2};
$display("dyn_array elements:",dyn_arr);
q1 = '{3,4};
$display("Queue elements:",q1);
q1 = '{default: 5};
$display("queue elements:",q1);
end
The following is the behavior without the -xlrm vsa_default_ap option:
Error-[IAP] Illegal assignment pattern

The following is the behavior with the -xlrm vsa_default_ap option:
%vcs -sverilog test.sv -xlrm vsa_default_ap
dyn_array elements:'{0, 0, 0}
dyn_array elements:'{2, 2, 2}
Queue elements:'{3, 4}
queue elements:'{5, 5}
Support for Reading Value of any Clock Variable
VCS supports reading the value of any clocking variable whose clock direction is output.
VCS supports this feature using the following compile-time option:
-xlrm relax_cb_dir
Example 140 test.sv
parameter simulation_cycle = 100;
bit SystemClock = 0;
kdb_mst_bfm_interface bfm_ifc(SystemClock);
test_top top_io(bfm_ifc);

#(simulation_cycle/2) SystemClock = ~SystemClock;
$display($realtime,,SystemClock);
end

Run the design using the following command:
%vcs -sverilog test.sv -full64 -xlrm relax_cb_dir
The output should not have any error messages.

Support for Virtual Interface Signals on Right-Hand Side (RHS) of
Force/Non Procedural Assignment
Starting with this release, VCS supports the force statement on Virtual Interface signals on
the Right-Hand Side (RHS) of Force/Non Procedural Assignment. This is an XLRM feature
supported under the option: -xlrm dyn_types_in_ca .
% vcs -sverilog <file_name> -xlrm dyn_types_in_ca
Example 141 test.sv
interface intf ();
logic din = 1;

interface intf1 ();
logic din1;
logic din2;

module t;
intf inst();
intf1 inst1();
virtual intf v_virt = inst;
assign inst1.din2 = v_virt.din;
force inst1.din1=v_virt.din;
#1 $display("din1 = %d, din2 = %d", inst1.din1, inst1.din2);
end
Behavior without the -xlrm dyn_types_in_ca option:
Error-[IUOVI] Incorrect use of virtual interface
test.sv 16
"v_virt.din"
Virtual interface variables are not permitted in this context.

Error-[IUOVI] Incorrect use of virtual interface
test.sv 14
"v_virt.din"
Virtual interface variables are not permitted in this context.

Behavior with the -xlrm dyn_types_in_ca option:
%vcs -sverilog test.sv -xlrm dyn_types_in_ca
din1 = 1, din2 = 1
The following are the limitations of the force statement:
• Usage of virtual interface signal on the Left-Hand Side (LHS) force statement is not
• Virtual interface signal in procedural continuous assignments is not supported.
