The following are the limitations with this functionality:
• Object handles returned by function calls cannot be used for comparison in constraint
guards.
• An array of objects cannot be used for comparison in constraint guards. Comparing
singleton members is supported. The following constraint specification results in a “Not
Yet Implemented” runtime error.
foreach (a_arry[j]) {
if (a_arry[j] != null) { x inside { [2:5] }; }
}
}
Error message:
Error-[NYI] Not Yet Implemented
orig_null.sv, 18
Feature is not yet supported: objects in object (in)equality must
currently refer to singleton members (no array elements, function
calls, null, )
Support for Functions Returning Class Object Instances in
Constraints
VCS supports functions returning class object instances in constraints.

Support for Functions Returning Class Object Instances in Constraints
VCS supports the following two scenarios:
• A function call returns a class object that directly refers to a function variable in
constraints. For example:
{ _tree.get_stuff(_tree._index) != null -> _tree._base < _main; }
• Reference to the class member whose object is returned by the function. For example:
{ _tree.get_stuff(_tree._index)._addr <= _tree._index; }
The following is the usage example:
module tiny;
class inner;
rand bit [7:0] _addr;
function new(bit [7:0] addr);
_addr = addr;

class mid;
rand int _main;
typedef bit [7:0] idx_t;
inner _stuff[idx_t];
rand bit [17:0] _base;
rand bit [7:0] _index;

function new;
_stuff[8] = new(8);
_stuff[1] = new(1);
_stuff[2] = new(2);
_stuff[3] = new(3);

function inner get_stuff(idx_t idx);
assert(_stuff.exists(idx));
get_stuff = _stuff[idx];

class outer;
rand bit [29:0] _main;
rand mid _tree;

function new;
_tree = new;
outer k;
k =new();
k.randomize() with {

Support for Pure Constraint Block
_tree._index inside {[3:19]};
// scenario 1:
_tree.get_stuff(_tree._index) != null -> _tree._base < _main;
// scenario 2:
_tree.get_stuff(_tree._index)._addr <= _tree._index;
};
end
• Reference to the class member, which is of array type, through function call is not
• Chaining of multiple function calls is not supported. For example: f().g().k().a
• Chaining of function calls with a class object is not supported. For example:
f().a.b.g().c
Support for Pure Constraint Block
You can specify pure constraint block as defined in the IEEE SystemVerilog LRM Std
1800™-2012 Section 18.5.2 “Constraint inheritance”. A pure constraint block, specified
with the pure keyword defines a constraint prototype in a virtual class. The constraint
implementation is provided when a non-virtual class is derived from the virtual class.
The constraint that overrides a pure constraint block can be declared using a constraint
block, a constraint prototype or an external constraint of the same name in the body of the
derived class.
Example 170 Pure Constraint Implementation in a Derived Class
virtual class B;
pure constraint t1;
pure constraint q1;

rand int z,z1;
randc int x,y;
rand bit a,b;

constraint t1{
x inside {2,3,5};
y inside {3,4,7};
z==x+y;
}

