The following simulation output is generated :
arr = '{'h4e, 'hb4}
• This feature is not supported for the dynamic MDAs and sub-arrays.
Support for System Function Calls
VCS supports usage of the following system function calls:
• $size() System Function Call
• $clog2() System Function Call
$size() System Function Call
VCS supports usage of $size() system function call in the constraint code.
Example 201 Example for the usage of $size()
rand bit [7:0] arr1[1:0];
rand bit [7:0] arr2[][2:0][3:0];
rand bit [7:0] size1, size2, size3;

constraint cons_size {
arr2.size == 2;

// returns 'arr1' dimension size as '2'
size1 == $size(arr1);

// returns 'arr2' second dimension size as '3'
size2 == $size(arr2[0]);

// returns 'arr2' third dimension size as '4'

Support for System Function Calls
size3 == $size(arr2[0], 2);
}

$display("size1 = %0d, size2 = %0d, size3 = %0d",
obj.size1, obj.size2, obj.size3);
end
$clog2() System Function Call
VCS supports usage of $clog2() system function call or integer math function in the
constraint scope as per IEEE SystemVerilog LRM Std 1800™-2012 Section 20.8.1
“Integer math functions”.
This significantly improves the verification productivity. You can use the $clog2() system
function call directly in the constraint code to get the minimal number of bits required to
store the value and to generate random stimulus based on the number of bits accordingly.
Consider the following example for the usage of $clog2():
class cls ;
rand bit [15:0] a, b, c;

constraint cons {
c == a >> $clog2(b);
b inside {3, 5, 14, 28, 46};
a == 16'habcd;
}
repeat(3) begin
$display("a = 'h%4h, b = 'd%-4d, $clog2(b) = 'd%0d, c =
'h%4h",
obj.a, obj.b, $clog2(obj.b), obj.c);
end
end
To run the example, use the following command:
% vcs -sverilog clog2.v

